            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Exploring Algebraic Identities"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.069,
            0.883,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "81"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.108,
            0.815,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "**Example 10:** Let us begin with $x^{2}+7x+12=x^{2}+(a+b)x+ab.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.133,
            0.886,
            0.211
          ],
          "content": "Comparing the coefficients of the x-term and the constant terms on both sides of the equation, we get $a + b = 7$ and $a b = 1 2$ . Note that this is only possible when $a = 3$ and $b = 4 ,$ or a = 4 and $b = 3$ . Thus, we choose one of these two possibilities and write the factors as"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.216,
            0.523,
            0.235
          ],
          "content": "$\\left( x + a \\right) \\left( x + b \\right)$ , that is, $\\left(x+3\\right)\\left(x+4\\right)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.24,
            0.86,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": "**Example 11:** Let us try to factor $x ^ { 2 } + 1 1 x + 3 0$ in a similar manner."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.345,
            0.269,
            0.689,
            0.29
          ],
          "content": "x^{2}+11x+30=x^{2}+(a+b)x+ab.",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.306,
            0.886,
            0.423
          ],
          "content": "This leads to $a + b = 11$ and $ab   =   30$ . We need to choose values of a and b appropriately, so that $a + b = 11$ and $a b = 3 0$ are both satisfied. What if we choose $a = 2$ and $b = 1 5 ?$ Or $a = 3$ and b = 10? Clearly these will not work as $a + b$ is not equal to 11 even though $ab = 30.\\; \\mathrm{S0}$ , we look at the factors of 30 and arrive at a = 5 and b = 6 or vice-versa. This way the x-term, 11x, can be split as $5 x + 6 x ,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.434,
            0.717,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Thus}, x^{2}+11x+30=x^{2}+(5+6)x+30=(x+5)(x+6)",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.469,
            0.886,
            0.567
          ],
          "content": "**Example 12:** In order to factor $X_{\\triangle}^{2} = 5X + 6,$ we first note that the coefficient of x is negative. Once again comparing $x ^ { 2 } \\mathrm { ~ - ~ } 5 x \\mathrm { ~ + ~ } 6$ with $x^{2}+(a+b)x+ab,$ , we get $a \\pm b = -5$ and $ab = 6$ . Note that these two equations can be satisfied together only when $a = - 2$ and $b = - 3$ or vice-versa."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.375,
            0.594,
            0.622,
            0.622
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exercise Set 4.4"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.646,
            0.748,
            0.667
          ],
          "content": "1. Fill in the blanks to complete the following identities:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.18,
            0.674,
            0.59,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "(i) $s^{2}-11s+24=(\\underline{\\hspace{2cm}})(\\underline{\\hspace{2cm}})$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.706,
            0.556,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": "(ii) $(x + 1) = (3x^2 - 4x - 7)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.737,
            0.586,
            0.757
          ],
          "content": "(iii) $10x^{2}-11x-6=(2x-\\frac{ 尨 }{ 尨 })(\\frac{ 尨 }{ 尨 }+2)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.768,
            0.654,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": "(iv) $6x^{2} + 7x + 2 = \\left( \\underline{\\hspace{2cm}} \\right)\\left( \\underline{\\hspace{2cm}} \\right)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.801,
            0.885,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": "2. Select and use the identity that will help you to find the following products without multiplying directly: (i) $( 4 1 ) ^ { 2 }$ (ii) $( 2 7 ) ^ { 2 }$ (iii) $( 2 3 \\times 1 7 )$ (iv) $( 1 3 5 ) ^ { 2 }$"
        }
      ]
